WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:02.435
-My first guest tonight
is an Emmy-nominated

00:00:02.435 --> 00:00:04.937
and three-time
Grammy-winning artist.

00:00:04.937 --> 00:00:08.141
He's also the founder of the
non-profit SocialWorks.

00:00:08.141 --> 00:00:12.212
Here is Chance the Rapper.
Yeah!

00:00:12.212 --> 00:00:13.512
That's what I'm talking about.
-Hey.

00:00:13.512 --> 00:00:17.083
-You look good, bud. You look
good. I missed you, man.

00:00:17.083 --> 00:00:18.417
-Oh, thanks, man.
You look good too.

00:00:18.417 --> 00:00:20.653
-I appreciate it.
-It's awesome to be back

00:00:20.653 --> 00:00:22.988
with you.
-Where are you calling us from?

00:00:22.988 --> 00:00:24.758
Looks like you're in
a recording studio?

00:00:24.758 --> 00:00:29.228
-Yeah, I'm at the studio,
just chilling.

00:00:29.228 --> 00:00:31.931
-Is this the studio where you
recorded all your stuff or no?

00:00:31.931 --> 00:00:35.969
-Yeah, this is CRC in Chicago.

00:00:35.969 --> 00:00:40.273
This is where I have been
recording here since 2014.

00:00:40.273 --> 00:00:44.143
I made "Coloring Book" here.
Most of my projects,

00:00:44.143 --> 00:00:48.214
my Christmas projects,
"Surf" this is the spot yeah.

00:00:48.214 --> 00:00:51.517
-When you first started out,
how did you afford studio time?

00:00:51.517 --> 00:00:56.522
-I was in high school when I
found this after school program.

00:00:56.522 --> 00:00:58.290
Or it found me, kinda.
I was walking down the street.

00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:03.429
And this guy came out and told
me about a free studio

00:01:03.429 --> 00:01:05.632
inside the library.

00:01:05.632 --> 00:01:08.835
And I came in and checked it out
and it was a really cool

00:01:08.835 --> 00:01:12.805
after school learning center
called Umedia,

00:01:12.805 --> 00:01:15.908
and the guy who brought me in
was called Brother Mike.

00:01:15.908 --> 00:01:17.977
He became my mentor.

00:01:17.977 --> 00:01:20.112
A lot of kids in Chicago,
a lot of people --

00:01:20.112 --> 00:01:21.648
well, they're adults now.

00:01:21.648 --> 00:01:24.650
but a lot of the people
that I work with

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:26.218
all came out of
that same program.

00:01:26.218 --> 00:01:32.058
Yeah, they had a free studio.
So I just -- yeah, right.

00:01:32.058 --> 00:01:36.495
-Have you always gone
by Chance the Rapper?

00:01:36.495 --> 00:01:40.066
-No.
So, well, it was --

00:01:40.066 --> 00:01:41.500
It actually,
when I was in high school,

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:45.872
I used to go by Chano.
It was a terrible name.

00:01:45.872 --> 00:01:48.641
And when Twitter first game out,

00:01:48.641 --> 00:01:52.311
this girl that was
a friend of mine,

00:01:52.311 --> 00:01:55.514
made a twitter for me and
made me Chance the Rapper

00:01:55.514 --> 00:01:59.152
and I was like, okay,
I will just go with that.

00:01:59.152 --> 00:02:01.688
-No way, I love hearing that.
-Yeah.

00:02:01.688 --> 00:02:04.557
-Do you remember when you wrote
your first rap?

00:02:04.557 --> 00:02:07.527
-Well, when I first started out,
I started making poetry first.

00:02:07.527 --> 00:02:09.629
Really I first started as a
dancer, right?

00:02:09.629 --> 00:02:12.932
And then fourth grade,
we did this thing called

00:02:12.932 --> 00:02:14.801
The Young Author's Project.

00:02:14.801 --> 00:02:16.035
But we did it every year,
and this year,

00:02:16.035 --> 00:02:19.272
I decided to do a book of poems,

00:02:19.272 --> 00:02:23.676
and I liked it and I had
never written poetry before.

00:02:23.676 --> 00:02:27.580
But I just -- out of nowhere, I
just decided to start doing it,

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:29.915
and then soon after that I fell
in love with hip-hop.

00:02:29.915 --> 00:02:34.487
And then I started recording
and when I was probably 14,

00:02:34.487 --> 00:02:38.224
My cousin had his own studio
that I got to go to

00:02:38.224 --> 00:02:40.192
once or twice and I recorded two
songs there.

00:02:40.192 --> 00:02:42.995
And I just always loved it
and always was like,

00:02:42.995 --> 00:02:45.197
this is going to be my thing.

00:02:45.197 --> 00:02:48.568
But the first rap I wrote was
probably -- I know what it was.

00:02:48.568 --> 00:02:52.271
It was a song called
the Black Definition.

00:02:52.271 --> 00:02:55.841
And I do not want to rap it
right now.

00:02:55.841 --> 00:02:58.945
But yeah I remember, I've been
writing songs for a long time,

00:02:58.945 --> 00:03:02.181
since I was a kid.
-Yeah, was everyone supportive

00:03:02.181 --> 00:03:05.284
of you growing up,
wanting to be a rapper?

00:03:05.284 --> 00:03:09.254
-Uh, I don't know if they were
supportive of it.

00:03:09.254 --> 00:03:10.489
But it was understand.

00:03:10.489 --> 00:03:13.459
It was a thing that people knew
that I rapped.

00:03:13.459 --> 00:03:15.628
Like, since I was a kid.
And that was just a thing

00:03:15.628 --> 00:03:18.631
I was into and I moved my hands
and stuff.

00:03:18.631 --> 00:03:26.338
I don't know. It was weird.
Yeah, my dad actually really --

00:03:26.338 --> 00:03:30.509
right when I got out of high
school, we kind of --

00:03:30.509 --> 00:03:33.813
you know, as parents do
when their kids graduate

00:03:33.813 --> 00:03:36.649
from high school and they don't
go to college or get a job,

00:03:36.649 --> 00:03:38.818
it's kind of like a
friction thing.

00:03:38.818 --> 00:03:43.723
-Sure.
-Yeah, but after we separated

00:03:43.723 --> 00:03:45.858
for a while, he came --
we got back together

00:03:45.858 --> 00:03:51.463
and he really helped, you know,
just guide me in terms of

00:03:51.463 --> 00:03:55.100
the amount of work and focus
that I needed to work on it

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:56.169
from a business side.

00:03:56.169 --> 00:03:59.005
And he still is doing that to
this day.

00:03:59.005 --> 00:04:03.309
But yeah, the beginning, my dad
did not want me to be a rapper.

00:04:03.309 --> 00:04:05.211
-It's like telling your parents
you want to be a comedian.

00:04:05.211 --> 00:04:10.149
They're like, no.
Anything but that.

00:04:10.149 --> 00:04:13.652
No, you can't do that.
But -- looking at you now, man.

00:04:13.652 --> 00:04:15.488
You have been crushing it.

00:04:15.488 --> 00:04:17.623
I got to say,
you never stopped working

00:04:17.623 --> 00:04:20.426
since I first was
introduced to you.

00:04:20.426 --> 00:04:22.995
You have a top-ten song
with Justin Bieber.

00:04:22.995 --> 00:04:26.866
You have done three virtual
concerts.

00:04:26.866 --> 00:04:30.303
The virtual concert,
this is amazing.

00:04:30.303 --> 00:04:31.404
It's cool that you're doing
these things.

00:04:31.404 --> 00:04:32.405
And thank you
for doing these things.

00:04:32.405 --> 00:04:35.174
-Thank you, man.
-You did one for --

00:04:35.174 --> 00:04:37.410
you said the mix was just for
car speakers.

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:40.880
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
It's like -- I don't know.

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:44.250
I have been taking this virtual
concert thing really seriously.

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:49.154
I put a lot of time into --
not just writing the story

00:04:49.154 --> 00:04:52.324
Around it or shooting it
but also in the mix.

00:04:52.324 --> 00:04:54.961
Just in the audio,
spending days and days

00:04:54.961 --> 00:04:58.330
trying to make it sound,
you know, robust

00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:02.902
And like a studio album versus
a big room or something.

00:05:02.902 --> 00:05:06.471
And so we mixed the last one
for the ride there,

00:05:06.471 --> 00:05:11.210
the one that's in the car.
Yeah, it's a mix, to be --

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:14.113
you know,
listened to in the car.

00:05:14.113 --> 00:05:17.816
It sounds good. Try it.
-I love that, man.

00:05:17.816 --> 00:05:19.585
That's taking it to the next
level.

00:05:19.585 --> 00:05:21.520
No I want it to sound great if
you're in a car.

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:24.056
This is perfect for this.

00:05:24.056 --> 00:05:26.692
I want to talk about some of the
other cool things you're doing,

00:05:26.692 --> 00:05:28.493
in addition to being
a talented artist,

00:05:28.493 --> 00:05:31.130
you're also a very good
activist.

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:37.236
You were named to billboard's
2020 R&amp;B Hip-Hop Power Players,

00:05:37.236 --> 00:05:40.272
in the year of activism,
congratulations on that.

00:05:40.272 --> 00:05:44.710
Your organization, SocialWorks
recently launched its

00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:47.913
Warmest Winter initiative.
What is that?

00:05:47.913 --> 00:05:51.317
-Yeah, so Warmest Winter is
actually something we have been

00:05:51.317 --> 00:05:54.387
doing for,
this is I think our fifth year,

00:05:54.387 --> 00:05:58.357
and it's basically
a series of events

00:05:58.357 --> 00:06:01.127
that start around November,
around right now,

00:06:01.127 --> 00:06:03.763
and go through
the beginning of March.

00:06:03.763 --> 00:06:06.865
And it's just a series of events
and initiatives that we do

00:06:06.865 --> 00:06:10.536
that target the homeless
and sometimes specifically

00:06:10.536 --> 00:06:12.371
the homeless youth in Chicago.

00:06:12.371 --> 00:06:16.008
And it's -- you know, we do
warming centers.

00:06:16.008 --> 00:06:18.977
There is always a big thing,
which we're pivoting this year,

00:06:18.977 --> 00:06:20.313
called the Night at the Museum.

00:06:20.313 --> 00:06:22.948
Where we have, like a huge --
we take over the

00:06:22.948 --> 00:06:26.852
Museum of Science and Industry,
and this year specifically,

00:06:26.852 --> 00:06:31.223
we added a couple events and
kind of pivoted away from

00:06:31.223 --> 00:06:34.693
some of the -- you know,
big person events.

00:06:34.693 --> 00:06:36.796
And we're doing actually a
Turkey give away.

00:06:36.796 --> 00:06:40.633
I think it's next weekend,
and yeah,

00:06:40.633 --> 00:06:42.535
we have a bunch of different
spots around the city

00:06:42.535 --> 00:06:46.272
that we're starting to do --
we've actually never taken

00:06:46.272 --> 00:06:47.573
part in the Turkey giveaways.

00:06:47.573 --> 00:06:49.041
We do a lot of
other kind of events.

00:06:49.041 --> 00:06:51.711
But we're trying to like start
it to it early

00:06:51.711 --> 00:06:54.446
and just give back,
because of the climate

00:06:54.446 --> 00:06:57.849
with the pandemic and
everything.

00:06:57.849 --> 00:06:59.785
-Dude, never stop, man.
I think it's so cool.

00:06:59.785 --> 00:07:02.321
You always, since day once,
you always give back.

00:07:02.321 --> 00:07:05.257
The Turkey give away, if you
want information on that go to

00:07:05.257 --> 00:07:09.561 line:0%
socialworkschi.org.

00:07:09.561 --> 00:07:12.131 line:0%
It is very, very cool what
you're doing. I love that.

00:07:12.131 --> 00:07:13.366 line:0%
Give away those Turkeys.
-Thanks, man.

